\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces One I$^2$SB run (10 steps), shown in both coordinates of Algorithm~\ref {alg:side}, with the same noise draws. \emph  {Top:} the bridge state $\bm  {x}_t$, which is what the network is fed; it starts at $\bm  {x}_1$ and its noise never exceeds $\tau $. \emph  {Middle:} the ascent state $\bm  {u}=(\bm  {x}_t-t\bm  {x}_1)/(1-t)$, the target plus noise $\sigma $. It is arbitrary at $\sigma =\infty $, and saturated noise (clipped for display) at the first finite levels. \emph  {Bottom:} the estimate, identical in both coordinates. The first estimate is the one-shot regression $\mathbb  {E}[\bm  {x}_0\mid \bm  {x}_1]$, and detail appears as $\sigma $ falls.}}{9}{figure.2}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:trajectory}{{2}{9}{One I$^2$SB run (10 steps), shown in both coordinates of Algorithm~\ref {alg:side}, with the same noise draws. \emph {Top:} the bridge state $\x _t$, which is what the network is fed; it starts at $\x _1$ and its noise never exceeds $\tau $. \emph {Middle:} the ascent state $\uu =(\x _t-t\x _1)/(1-t)$, the target plus noise $\sigma $. It is arbitrary at $\sigma =\infty $, and saturated noise (clipped for display) at the first finite levels. \emph {Bottom:} the estimate, identical in both coordinates. The first estimate is the one-shot regression $\E [\x _0\mid \x _1]$, and detail appears as $\sigma $ falls}{figure.2}{}}
